    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## What the change does\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` opt-in (allowing an owner at an address already used by a same-lastName owner past the `409` household-duplicate guard) was already wired up in `rejectDuplicateHousehold`. The missing piece was the **shared `householdId`**, which I added.\n\n## Approach\n\nI made `householdId` a **deterministic, stable function of the household** \u2014 the SHA-256 hex of the normalized `lastName` + `address` comparison key. Because it's derived purely from those fields (via the same case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsing rule used for duplicate detection), every owner in the same household independently derives the *same* identifier with no coordination or mutation of existing records, and it stays stable across restarts. That's exactly what the test asserts: two owners joining an existing household get equal, non-blank `householdId` values.\n\n## Files changed\n\n- **`util/HouseholdNormalizer.java`** \u2014 added `toHouseholdId(lastName, address)`, a pure function returning the hex SHA-256 of the existing comparison key.\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter.\n- **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 `addOwner` assigns `householdId` before save; `updateOwner` recomputes it so it stays consistent if `lastName`/`address` change.\n- **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `householdId` (read-only string) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically. `sharesHousehold` was already present on `OwnerFields`.\n- **`resources/db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `household_id` column.\n\n## Tests\n\n`mvn test` is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` passes (two `sharesHousehold` joiners at the same address/last name return an equal, non-blank `householdId`), and the full suite builds successfully.",
